  "worked_solution": "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}y-{2}^{y} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-{2}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({2}^{y}) & =6x \\\\ (1-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2){2}^{y})(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =6x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(1-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)\\,{2}^{y}) & =6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6x}{1-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)\\,{2}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
